A||||13||CC Rider||ignition module failures||||1||
Z||000000||CC Rider||05-16-2001||03:48 PM||||So can you pull the ignition module out of the distributor and place it in an area that would be cooler ? <BR>If not, is there a way to cool it better ? Vent tuge of some type ?<P>Is the module building up the heat or is it the heat transfer from the distributor heating up the module ?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Ken Wittlief||05-16-2001||04:15 PM||||there already is a blower that puts air across it when the radiator fan comes on, thats what those silve tubes are in the engine compartment.<P>Also the engine is pretty open back there, at 60mph you get a pretty good blast of air all over the engine.<P>so I dont know if you can really make much temp difference by moving it, unless you idle your car or drive in 3mph gridlock alot.<P> you could wire the blower back there so its on all the time if it makes you feel better.||||reg||1
Z||000002||rogergarrison||05-16-2001||04:36 PM||||make sure you coat the module good with white dialelectric grease. It helps to isolate it from heat transfered up the distributor shaft. I have 80k on mine now, only replaced it once.||||reg||1
Z||000003||CC Rider||05-16-2001||05:04 PM||||So your saying there is a blower in the engine compartment that turn on when the raidiator fan turns on ? Where is this mounted ?<P>I knew the silver tubes at the back wall of the engine comp. were cooling vents but did not know they were fan driven.<P>My real question is could the life of the module be extended if it operates at a lower temp and what is hotter, the distibutor or the module at running temp.||||reg||1
Z||000004||87GTZ34||05-16-2001||05:35 PM||||The main means of heat dissipation for the ignition module is the silicone grease that xfers heat from the module to the dist housing. This design is adequate for normal operation of the car as Roger & Ken mentioned.  If you are getting external heat soak into the module and killing them, then something else is wrong.  What I mean is if you have hot after market exhaust, that may well be the culprit.  But normal mid summertime underhood temps alone won't kill the module.  Also, I think some people have had bad experiences with non-OEM replacements not lasting very long.  GL||||reg||1
Z||000005||CC Rider||05-16-2001||05:36 PM||||rogergarrison :<P>So you say that the distributor is hotter then the Module and that the white dialelectric grease is a block to the heat transfer. <P>Correct ?||||reg||1
Z||000006||cadaver||05-16-2001||05:39 PM||||I tried an NON GM module. It sucked. I have 178XXX on mine and only gone through two modules. Thats not to bad. And its been 15 years. How much better do you want?||||reg||1
Z||000007||87GTZ34||05-16-2001||06:04 PM||||Actually, air is a better heat xfer insulator.  The module is screwed into the dist housing at the two endpoints and there are 2 positioning dowels.  If no grease is used the heat buildup in the module must exit in 2 small areas (the screws) causing uneven heat distribution and slowing heat dissipation.  The grease allows for heat xfer throughout the module surface allow a higher rate of xfer.  My guess is if you built an extension harness and removed the module and didn't mount it to anything, it would fry in minutes and when you touch it right after it died, you would scar your hand for life.  It gets very hot.||||reg||1
Z||000008||cadaver||05-16-2001||08:52 PM||||Oh transfer||||reg||1
Z||000009||Formula88||05-17-2001||12:04 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by CC Rider:<BR><B>rogergarrison :<P>So you say that the distributor is hotter then the Module and that the white dialelectric grease is a block to the heat transfer. <P>Correct ?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>No.  The grease is a conductor of heat and helps the heat sink from the module into the distributor so it'll dissipate better.  Without it the module will overheat and burn out easier.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Oreif||05-17-2001||12:12 PM||||Use a THIN coat of grease. The thicker you put on the grease the more it will decrease the heat transfer by insulating the module.<BR>The ignition module will get hotter than the distributor. We had one where the mounting screws were broke. The thing baked itself within 2 hours.<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">   <BR>Got Emblems?||||reg||1
Z||000011||Ken Wittlief||05-17-2001||01:53 PM||||the blower is in the rear trunk, upper passenger side, behind the carpet.||||reg||1
Z||000012||CC Rider||05-17-2001||02:57 PM||||So here is the definition of the word Dielectric :--&gt; insulator, dielectric, nonconductor -- (a material such as glass or porcelain with negligible electrical or thermal conductivity) <P>The part that caught my eye was : negligible electrical or thermal conductivity.<P>I have talked to a few of my friends who are engineers about dielectric grease and was told that using dielectric grease will inhibit the thermal transfer to the distributor body. <P>Should we be using a Thermal Grease that you can get at any electronic store ?  This grease is designed to transfer heat from one part to the next.||||reg||1
Z||000013||theogre||05-19-2001||01:56 PM||||In this case......<P>Dielectric grease is an esentially inert Si compound. It has 3 main uses in cars.<P>1. keep water out of connections.<BR>2. electircally insulate connections from conductive dirt.<BR>3. as a thermal transfer agent on parts such as ignition modules.<P>If you look in the tech area of my cave theres an old article on CPU heatsinks. <A HREF="http://ogrecave.shorturl.com" TARGET=_blank>The Ogre's Cave</A>The info there applies here... In short,,,<P>Thermal Transfer Agent, AKA Heatsink Compound, is 4X better than plain dielectric and 20X better than air as measured/stated by Thermalloy.<P>Most heatsink compound is Si dielectric mixed with Zinc Oxide. There are other compounds but you likely can't get them for public consumption.<P>So....... As odd as is sounds, at the very least you want to put a bit of dielectric in there. Heatsink coumpound would be better but the results may not be noticable.<P><BR>Don't forget to read the heat managment article in my fiero tunnel for a more detailed coverage.... <A HREF="http://fierocave.shorturl.com" TARGET=_blank>The Ogre's Fiero Cave</A>||||reg||1
